`std::system` on Windows generally uses `cmd.exe` to execute the given command string. Your code snippet has a few issues that could make it fail on Windows:

1. For `_WIN64`, you're using `"start"`, but you should use `"start \"\""` to avoid treating the URL as the title of the new Command Prompt window.

2. For `_WIN32`, you're using `"explorer"` which should work, but make sure to compile for the correct architecture (`_WIN32` or `_WIN64`).

Here's a corrected version:

```cpp
void open_url(const std::string &url) {
    std::string executable;

#if defined(_WIN32) || defined(_WIN64)
    executable = "start \"\"";
#elif defined(__linux__)
    executable = "xdg-open";
#elif defined(__APPLE__)
    executable = "open";
#elif defined(__ANDROID__)
    executable = "am start -a android.intent.action.VIEW -d";
#endif

    const std::string command = executable + " \"" + url + "\"";

    const int exitcode = std::system(command.c_str());
    if (exitcode != 0) {
        // debugmsg("Failed to open URL: %s\nAttemped command was: %s", url, command);
        // Replace debugmsg with your debugging/logging function
    }
}
```

Make sure you are using the right compile-time definitions (`_WIN32` or `_WIN64`) for your build.

